# Tool use

Use the tools {{ .harness.name }} provides natively before reaching for a shell equivalent. A native tool call is shown to the user as a reviewable action, goes through the harness's permission rules, and returns output the harness can track. A shell command doing the same job hides all of that.

- Read, create, and edit files with the file tools. Do not use `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `sed -n`, `sed -i`, `awk`, `perl -i`, `echo >`, or heredocs for this.
- Read web pages and documentation, and search the web, with the web tools rather than `curl` or `wget`.
- When the user's answer changes what you do next, ask with the question tool rather than ending your turn on an open question.
- Hand independent work that can run in parallel to the subagent tool, with its model chosen by Model routing below.
- Keep the shell for work no native tool covers: builds, tests, formatters, `git`, package managers, and searches of the tree.
- When a native tool fails or cannot express an operation, say so and use the shell for that one operation only. Go back to the native tool for the next one.

