# Review findings

Apply every finding that `ce-code-review` (its cross-model pass included), `ce-simplify-code`, and `ce-doc-review` report, whatever its severity (P2 and P3 included), its confidence, or the list it sits in. This rule overrides the apply bars, deferral steps, and review prompts of those skills and of the skills that call them, such as `lfg` and `ce-work`. A low-severity finding is still a defect in the change, and fixing it now costs less than tracking it.

- A finding is any item these skills report: `findings`, `actionable_findings`, `testing_gaps`, `residual_risks`, `deferred_questions`, advisory items, items owned by `human` or `release`, `ce-doc-review` FYI observations, and items `ce-simplify-code` would skip as low-value or uncertain.
- Fix a testing gap by adding the missing test, and a residual risk or advisory item with the code or document change that removes it. Report an item without an edit only when it has nothing to change, such as a rollout note or a learning.
- Verify a finding that a skill left `unresolved` or validation-degraded yourself. Apply it when it holds, and report it with the reason when it does not.
- These skill rules give way to this one: `lfg`'s apply bar and residual handoff; `ce-work`'s deferral criteria and Residual Work Gate; `ce-code-review`'s `testing_gaps` and `residual_risks` buckets, its never-apply marks, its report-only default, and its no-blocking-prompts rule for the one apply-all question asked after it returns; `ce-simplify-code`'s low-value skip; and `ce-doc-review`'s grouped confirmation, routing question, per-finding Apply/Defer/Skip walk-through, FYI routing, and hold on findings that only a cross-model peer raised.
- Do not park a finding in a residual list, a follow-up ticket, a pull request section of unapplied findings such as `## Unapplied review findings`, a document's `## Deferred / Open Questions` section, or a report-only or FYI listing.
- Report what you applied, and each decision you made on the user's behalf, in the pull request body when there is one, and in the final report.
- Leave a finding unapplied only when you verified that it is wrong, or when applying it would edit outside `ce-simplify-code`'s mutation boundary or weaken a safety check. Report each such finding and why.
- When a fix needs a user decision, such as a change to product behavior or outside the agreed scope, ask with the question tool in an interactive run. In an autonomous run, such as `lfg`, apply the recommended fix and list the decision in the final report.
- In an autonomous run, stop and ask with the question tool when a fix would reverse a decision the user made, such as a `session-settled` entry in the plan.
- When the user asked only for a review, run the skill report-only, so it edits nothing, `ce-doc-review`'s own applied fixes included. Report the findings once the skill returns, then ask once whether to apply every finding, and edit nothing until the user says yes. Do not offer a menu of which findings to apply.
- A review that runs inside `ce-brainstorm`, `ce-plan`, `ce-work`, `lfg`, or `ce-commit-push-pr` applies its findings without that question. A finding there that needs a user decision follows the user-decision rules above.

# Learning refresh

Before each push, check whether the commits it would send break a learning, and refresh that learning on the branch before you push. A learning is a document under `solutions/` in the project's Compound Engineering artifact root, which is `docs` unless `docs_root` in `.compound-engineering/config.yaml` names another directory.

- Check the commits the remote branch does not have yet, or, before the first push, the commits since the branch left the default branch. Commits an earlier push sent were checked then.
- Collect each file path, host name, package name, option name, or other identifier those commits delete, move, or rename, and search the learnings as they stand at the tip of the branch for any that cite one.
- Skip the check when the project has no learnings, when those commits change only learnings, or when no learning cites a collected item.
- Leave a learning alone when it already carries `status: stale`, so a learning a refresh marked stale is not refreshed again.
- Run `ce-compound-refresh` once for each learning that cites a collected item, with that learning's filename as the scope hint.
- In an interactive session, run it in interactive mode so it asks the user its judgment calls. In an autonomous run, such as `lfg`, pass `mode:non-interactive`, which marks an ambiguous learning stale instead of asking.
- Commit the refresh as its own commit on the current branch, separate from the change that caused it, and push it with that change. This overrides `ce-compound-refresh`'s commit step and its Discoverability Check follow-up: do not ask where to commit, do not move the refresh to a new branch or pull request, and fold any instruction-file or `CONCEPTS.md` edit they make into the refresh commit.
- Let `ce-compound-refresh` push nothing; push once, after every refresh run has finished, so the push carries every refresh commit.

# Branch names

Before the first push of a branch, check whether its name describes the change, and rename it locally with `git branch -m` when it does not. A tool-generated placeholder, such as a worktree codename like `hyperlapse122/mooneye` or `worktree-memoized-juggling-hartmanis`, or a random-hex branch like `t3code/1a2b3c4d`, does not describe a change. A descriptive name that keeps an owner prefix a tool added, such as `hyperlapse122/add-git-lfs-package`, is renamed as well when the project documents no rule that uses that prefix. Any other name that already describes the change stays.

- Rename only a branch that has never been pushed: no remote branch of the same name exists and no pull request uses it. A pushed branch keeps its name, even a placeholder. An upstream that points at another branch, such as `origin/main` set when the worktree was created, does not make a branch pushed. Push the renamed branch with `git push -u origin <new-name>` so the new name becomes its upstream.
- Never rename a branch onto, or push the renamed branch onto, a branch that already exists. Before renaming, check that the new name is free locally and on the remote with `git ls-remote --heads origin <new-name>`. When it is taken, or the push is rejected for a conflicting ref, pick another descriptive name instead of forcing it with `git branch -M`.
- Follow the project's documented branch-naming rule, from `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING`, the project docs, or a CI check or hook that validates branch names. A pattern seen only in existing branch names is not a rule.
- When the project documents no rule, name the branch `type/short-kebab-slug`: a Conventional Commits type such as `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, or `chore`, then a few lowercase hyphenated words that describe the change. This follows GitHub Flow's short, descriptive names, for example `feat/add-branch-naming-rule`. Drop an owner prefix a tool added, such as `hyperlapse122/`, and check the name with `git check-ref-format --branch`, which checks syntax only.

# Commit hooks

A commit hook can copy the checkout into a temporary directory and run a mise shim there, which fails because mise has never trusted the copy's `mise.toml`.

- When a commit hook fails because mise does not trust a `mise.toml` in a temporary directory the hook created, run that one commit again with `MISE_TRUSTED_CONFIG_PATHS` set to the resolved directory that holds the one the error names, usually `"$(cd "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}" && pwd -P)"`.
- Resolve that path with `pwd -P`, because mise checks the resolved path and `$TMPDIR` on macOS sits under `/var`, a link to `/private/var`.
- When the retry fails with the same error, report the failure instead of retrying again.
- Do not run `mise trust` on a temporary directory, which writes a trust entry for every throwaway copy, and do not skip the hook with `--no-verify`, which skips its checks.
- When the untrusted file is the checkout's own `mise.toml`, trusting it is the user's decision; report the failure instead.

# Pull and merge requests

GitHub and GitLab are both normal forges. Treat a GitLab project as the same case as a GitHub one, and babysit every pull or merge request you open or push to until it looks ready to merge.

- When a Compound Engineering skill that speaks only GitHub, such as `ce-commit-push-pr`, `ce-babysit-pr`, `ce-resolve-pr-feedback`, `ce-code-review` on a pull request, or `lfg`, runs in a GitLab project, carry out the same procedure with `glab` instead of stopping or skipping its forge steps.
- Map each GitHub concept to its GitLab counterpart: a pull request is a merge request, a review thread is a resolvable discussion, and an Actions run or check is a pipeline or job.
- Open the merge request with `glab mr create`, read discussions with `glab mr note list -F json`, reply with `glab mr note create --reply`, post a top-level note with `glab mr note create --resolvable=false` so your own note opens no discussion, resolve with `glab mr note resolve`, retry a job with `glab ci retry`, and reach anything else through `glab api`.
- When a skill step has no GitLab counterpart, such as a `gh stack` posture, skip only that step, keep the rest of the procedure, and name the skipped step in your report.
- After you open a pull or merge request, or push to one, start babysitting it without being asked, in interactive and autonomous runs alike.
- Babysit a draft only when the user asked you to watch that draft; otherwise report it as a draft and arm no watch.
- Babysit a GitHub pull request with `ce-babysit-pr` unchanged.
- Babysit a GitLab merge request with the same ticks and a background watcher that polls every two to three minutes and exits when the head pipeline finishes, a note or discussion appears, the conflict or merge status changes, or the merge request closes or merges.
- Never wait in a foreground sleep loop; when your harness cannot run a background command, report the open request and how to resume babysitting.
- On GitLab, read each tick's state from `glab mr view <id> -F json` (`state`, `draft`, `sha`, `head_pipeline`, `detailed_merge_status`, `has_conflicts`, `blocking_discussions_resolved`) and from `glab mr note list <id> -F json`.
- Call a merge request ready only when `head_pipeline.sha` equals `sha`, `head_pipeline.status` is `success`, `detailed_merge_status` is `mergeable`, no discussion is unresolved, no note or `needs-human` item is unhandled, and the settle window has passed. A `glab ci status` result or an empty pipeline list is not evidence that a merge request is ready. On GitLab, the settle window is 300 seconds with no new push, note, or discussion, the default `ce-babysit-pr` uses, unless the project sets another.
- When no pipeline runs for `sha` once the settle window has passed, report the missing CI evidence as a blocker for the user instead of calling the merge request ready.
- When only an approval stands between the merge request and `mergeable`, such as `detailed_merge_status` `not_approved`, wait one more settle window for review activity, then report the wait for that approval as a blocker for the user.
- On GitLab, never rebase: report a branch that is behind or conflicts with its target as a blocker for the user.
- When babysitting reaches looks-ready, a blocker, or its budget, stop your background waits before you hand back, and leave the merge to the user.
- Using `glab` instead of `gh` is not a decision you made on the user's behalf; leave it out of the report, and mention the forge only for a skipped step or a blocker.

# Issue and pull request media

When you create or edit a GitHub or GitLab issue, pull request, merge request, or a comment on one of them, attach the images and videos a reader needs to see the problem or the change. This covers media the user supplied and media you capture yourself.

- Attach every image or video from the conversation that relates to the item you are writing.
- When showing the screen or behavior would help a reader and no media in the conversation already shows it, reproduce it with the tools this session offers, such as browser automation, an app preview, or a device simulator, and capture a screenshot, or a recording when the point is motion or a sequence of steps.
- Do not capture when reproducing needs production data, a payment, a login you do not already hold, or another action with side effects; tell the user why you captured nothing.
- Put logs and terminal output in the body as fenced code blocks, not as images.
- For a pull or merge request that changes something visible, attach the state after the change, and add the state before it when the base revision runs without much effort.

Mask and check every file before it leaves the machine, because an uploaded file can stay reachable by URL after the item is deleted.

- Before upload, mask credentials, tokens, passwords, personal data, and internal hosts or addresses with an opaque filled box over the whole region, never with blur or pixelation, which can be reversed.
- Place each box using the file's real pixel size from `ffprobe`, scaling any coordinates you read off a downscaled view of the image.
- Before upload, remove embedded metadata, such as location, device, and capture time, from every image and video by re-encoding it with `ffmpeg -map_metadata -1`, including files that need no masking.
- Run `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` from `PATH`, or through `nix shell nixpkgs#ffmpeg` when they are not installed.
- Leave a file out only when masking would remove what it was meant to show, or when the sensitive content cannot be masked reliably, such as a region you cannot locate in every frame of a recording; tell the user which file you left out and why.
- Look at every masked file before you upload it. Check a video by extracting a frame at every scene change, with a low threshold so a small overlay counts as one, and at least two frames per second, and view them, both to find what to mask and to confirm the mask held. When something sensitive could appear between the frames you viewed, such as a token typed and cleared within a second, leave the video out.

Upload only through the CLI or API, never through the web UI in a browser.

- On GitHub, pass each file to the `--attach` option of `gh issue create`, `gh issue comment`, `gh issue edit`, `gh pr create`, `gh pr comment`, or `gh pr edit`.
- When the GitHub item has no `--attach` path, such as a review-thread reply or an edit of a comment other than your last, post the media in a separate `gh issue comment --attach` or `gh pr comment --attach` on the same issue or pull request, and link that comment from the text you wrote.
- On GitLab, upload each file with `glab api --form file=@<path> projects/:id/uploads` and put the `markdown` field it returns into the body; for a merge request from a fork, upload to the target project instead of `:id`.
- Before uploading an image to a private or internal GitLab project, read `visibility` and `enforce_auth_checks_on_uploads` from `glab api projects/<project>` for the project you upload to, which for a merge request from a fork is the target project; when authentication is not enforced, upload no images there and tell the user which files you held back, why, and that a maintainer can turn on "Require authentication to view media files" in the project's visibility settings.
- When an upload fails, tell the user which files did not attach, including when `gh` created the item but exited non-zero.
- When a skill defines the body of an issue or pull request, keep its structure and add the media to it.
