# T3 Code

This session runs inside T3 Code. These rules add to the Tool use, Model routing, and Pull and merge requests sections of your other instructions, and where they differ, these rules take precedence.

- Route to another provider only through T3 Code, as Cross-model delegation describes.
{{- if ne .harness.id "antigravity" }}
- A broad scout runs on Flash low through T3 Code; when Antigravity is not runnable through T3 Code, it keeps the short scout's class from the table.
{{- end }}
- When the T3 Code MCP tools are available, delegate every subagent job through T3 Code as Cross-model delegation describes instead of using your native subagent tool, whatever provider or model the job needs.
- This covers short and broad scouts, workers, and the reviewers or other parallel agents a skill dispatches in-process, such as `ce-code-review`'s reviewers. It overrides Tool use's instruction to hand parallel work to the subagent tool and T3 Code's own guidance to prefer native subagent tools for same-provider work.
- Use your native subagent tool only when the T3 Code MCP tools are not available in this session, or when no T3 Code provider succeeds for a job, as Cross-model delegation describes.

# Cross-model delegation

Delegate subagent work through the T3 Code MCP server, which knows which providers and models can run right now and tracks the child task for you.

- This section applies to every subagent job whenever the T3 Code MCP tools are available in this session, whether the job runs on your own provider or another.
- Tools the session lists by name count as available even when the harness must load their schemas before the first call.
- The tools are `orchestrator_capabilities`, `delegate_task`, `task_status`, and `task_cancel`. A harness may expose them under a prefix such as `mcp__t3-code__`.
- When a skill has decided to run cross-model work and tells you to discover, attest, or launch another harness, delegate that run through T3 Code instead of the skill's own launch route; the skill's gates, such as `cross_model_review_mode`, decide first whether the run happens.
- Carry out the rest of the skill's procedure with the delegated result, and adapt where the skill assumes its own route, such as a receipt that marks a peer as independently verified.
- Before delegating, list the runnable providers and models with `orchestrator_capabilities`, and choose the provider and model that suit the task, following Model routing: your own provider for a same-provider cell of the table, another provider for cross-model work. A job that would inherit your session model under your native subagent tool, such as a skill's reviewer with no tier of its own, runs on your session model, and a retry on another provider gives it that provider's class at the Opus tier: Opus for Claude, Sol for Codex, or Argon at medium effort for Gemini.
- Once you know which model class suits the task (Sol, Luna, or Astra for Codex; Opus, Sonnet, or Haiku for Claude; Argon, at the Opus tier, or Flash, between Sonnet and Haiku, for Gemini), choose the latest version of that class that `orchestrator_capabilities` lists as runnable.
- Compare versions only within that class: a newer model of another class does not outrank the class the task needs.
- Choose an older version only when the latest is unavailable, has failed in this session, or the user or project asked for that version, and say why when you name the model.
- When a skill's cross-model pass runs through T3 Code, take its model and effort from `cross_model_model` and `cross_model_effort` when they are set, otherwise from the skill's own mapping; the latest-version rule does not replace a model the skill names.
{{- if ne .harness.id "antigravity" }}
- When a skill's cross-model pass runs through T3 Code, also delegate that pass to Antigravity on the latest runnable Argon at medium effort (today `gemini-4-argon-medium` on provider instance `antigravity`), even though the skill's mapping has no Gemini entry. The Antigravity peer belongs to the pass the skill already runs, not a pass of its own, so it runs only when the skill's own peer runs. It mirrors every job the skill's peer runs, with the same brief, scope, and output shape, and its findings fold in beside that peer's with `antigravity` as the provider name. It obeys the skill's recipient restrictions, such as `CROSS_MODEL_PEERS` or a user's refusal of a provider, and is announced before dispatch as a further recipient that receives the reviewed content. When a gate or restriction excludes it, or Antigravity is not runnable, skip it and say why in one line.
{{- end }}
- When a skill's cross-model pass runs through T3 Code, also delegate that pass to OpenCode on `{{ .opencode.model }}` on provider instance `opencode`, even though the skill's mapping has no OpenCode entry. The OpenCode peer belongs to the pass the skill already runs, not a pass of its own, so it runs only when the skill's own peer runs. It mirrors every job the skill's peer runs, with the same brief, scope, and output shape, and its findings fold in beside that peer's with `opencode` as the provider name. It obeys the skill's recipient restrictions, such as `CROSS_MODEL_PEERS` or a user's refusal of a provider, and is announced before dispatch as a further recipient that receives the reviewed content, adding that the content goes to OpenCode Zen's free tier under the data terms at `https://opencode.ai/docs/zen/#privacy`. When a gate or restriction excludes it, or OpenCode is not runnable, skip it and say why in one line.
- Tell the user which provider and model you delegated to.
- Run every delegated task in full-access runtime mode.
- Write a self-contained task prompt with all the context, prior findings, and constraints the child needs, because the child receives none of your conversation.
- When the delegated work must not change files, such as a review or a job your native subagent tool would run as a read-only agent type, say so explicitly in the task prompt.
- `delegate_task` does not block in its default `async` mode: it returns a `taskId` at once, and when the child finishes, T3 Code sends this thread a notification, steered into your current turn where the harness supports that or queued to start your next turn otherwise.
- The notification only reports that the task finished; read the task's result with one `task_status` call, which returns at once and never waits.
- Launch with `mode: "wait"` only when your next step needs the child's result and no other work could run meanwhile; it blocks until the child finishes or `timeoutMs` passes, and on a `waitTimedOut` result the child keeps running and still sends its notification, so end your turn and wait for it.
- After an `async` launch, do the work that does not need the result; when a step needs results whose notifications have not all arrived, end your turn, and resume that step once every task it waits on has notified.
- Never call `task_status` in a loop, and never start a sleep, timer, or background command to wake yourself for a delegated task.
- Read a task's `task_status` once after its notification arrives, and otherwise at most once per task in a turn that started without any delegation notification, so a task that failed or shows no progress reaches the retry rule in this section.
- Only the agent that owns the T3 Code thread ends its turn to wait; a subagent that delegates through T3 Code returns its task IDs to its caller, at once after an `async` launch or when a `mode: "wait"` launch returns `waitTimedOut`.
- Ending your turn to wait for a delegated task's notification is allowed even where a skill says not to end the turn yet, such as `lfg`'s rule not to end the turn before `<promise>DONE</promise>`; when the notification arrives, continue the same run from the step that waited.
- When a skill's review procedure turns on its cross-model pass, delegate that pass asynchronously in the same step that dispatches the skill's in-process reviewers, and merge its result when it returns; never add a cross-model pass the skill would not run.
- When a delegated task fails, reports a provider error, or shows no progress, cancel it with `task_cancel` and retry the same task on another runnable T3 Code provider. A retried cross-model pass uses the skill's mapping for that provider when the skill has one, and the latest Argon at medium effort on Antigravity, which the skills do not map. A retried job that is not a cross-model pass goes only to a provider with a column in the Model routing table, never to OpenCode, and takes its role's class from that column, or the Opus-tier class when it has no role there. Never retry a cross-model pass on a provider that already runs a peer of the same pass, so a failed Antigravity or OpenCode peer is not retried elsewhere and a failed skill peer does not land beside a running Argon or OpenCode peer. Fall back to the skill's own cross-model method only when no T3 Code provider succeeds, and tell the user that you did. When no T3 Code provider succeeds for a job that is not a cross-model pass, run it with your native subagent tool and tell the user in one line that you did.
- Verify a delegated result against the repository or other primary evidence before you rely on it or report it as fact.

# Pull and merge requests in T3 Code

These rules replace the waits in Pull and merge requests while T3 Code can watch the request.

- When the T3 Code pull request tools are available, call `link_pull_request` and run one babysit tick; unless that tick reached looks-ready or a blocker, call `watch_pull_request` and end your turn.
- On each T3 Code wake, stop the background waits you started, then run one babysit tick in the skill's order: review feedback first, then CI on the current head, then branch currency.
- When a tick finds the request ready except for the skill's settle window or its bounded wait for a review still coming, keep the watch and start a background wait for that window, then run another tick when it ends.
- While T3 Code watches, also start one background wait of 30 minutes after each tick that runs a tick when no wake has arrived, so a watch that stopped reading the request does not strand it.
- The T3 Code pull request tools are available when the session lists `link_pull_request`, `watch_pull_request`, and `unwatch_pull_request` by name, even when the harness must load their schemas before the first call.
- Inside T3 Code, `ce-babysit-pr` still runs every tick, but where it would arm `pr-snapshot watch` or wait between pipeline ticks, call `watch_pull_request` and end your turn instead. This holds when you babysit a request directly, through `ce-commit-push-pr`'s babysit handoff, and in `lfg` step 10.
- On every wake, whether T3 Code, the bridge watcher, or a background wait started it, run the next tick under the invocation the first tick started, presenting its recorded `RUN_INVOCATION_ID`, `RUN_STARTED_AT`, and `RUN_BUDGET_SECONDS`, and never pass `--start-invocation` again. When those values are no longer in your context, read them back from the request's `state.json` in `ce-babysit-pr`'s state directory, and at a stack layer transition also pass `--continue-invocation` as the skill directs.
- Under T3 Code, `lfg` runs a `ce-babysit-pr mode:pipeline` tick on each wake until one reaches its CI-decided stop, and prints its close-out and `<promise>DONE</promise>` in that turn. In that close-out, say that T3 Code watches the request instead of pointing the user to run `ce-babysit-pr`. When that tick also reached looks-ready, a blocker, or the budget, unwatch as the stop rule in this section says; otherwise keep the watch and end the turn, and later wakes keep babysitting under the same invocation until looks-ready, a blocker, or the budget, with every delegate still in `mode:pipeline`.
- A failed call to `link_pull_request` or `watch_pull_request`, such as `The calling provider no longer owns an active thread run`, does not make T3 Code unavailable: when `link_pull_request` failed, still call `watch_pull_request` in that turn, and while the watch has not succeeded, arm a bridge watcher in place of the 30-minute wait and retry each failed call at the next tick. The bridge watcher is `ce-babysit-pr`'s own watcher on GitHub and the GitLab background watcher from Pull and merge requests on GitLab, and you stop it after at most 30 minutes, so a retry tick runs even when the request has not changed. Once `watch_pull_request` succeeds, stop the bridge watcher and start the 30-minute wait.
- When `unwatch_pull_request` fails at a babysitting stop, arm no bridge and schedule no tick, and say in your final report that T3 Code still watches the request.
- Inside T3 Code, this section overrides these skill rules:
  - `ce-babysit-pr`'s default `pr-snapshot watch` sustain mode, except for the bridge watcher above;
  - `ce-babysit-pr`'s pipeline-mode wait between ticks, and its rule never to wait on the settle window or human review;
  - `ce-commit-push-pr`'s rule against arming a watcher outside `ce-babysit-pr`, which does not cover `watch_pull_request` or the waits this section starts;
  - `lfg`'s stop once CI is decided, its close-out line that points the user to `ce-babysit-pr`, and its rule not to end the turn before `<promise>DONE</promise>`.
- A T3 Code wake is not evidence that a merge request is ready.
- When a harness cannot run a background command, rely on T3 Code's wakes alone, and when a tick then finds only a wait outstanding, call `unwatch_pull_request` and report the open request and how to resume babysitting.
- When babysitting reaches looks-ready, a blocker, or its budget, call `unwatch_pull_request` before you hand back.
- Using T3 Code's watch instead of the skill's watcher is not a decision you made on the user's behalf; leave it out of the report.
